A||||5||Carrolles||VSS How many pulses per mile?||||1
Z||000000||Carrolles||10-21-1999||10:05 AM||||Cliff, Ogre, Anybody,<P>I am installing a JC Whitney VSS cruise control and need to know how many pulses per mile the VSS unit puts out on my 85SE v-6 4spd.  I might be able to find this info in the Helms manual but I don't have it with me.<P>The choices on the cruise control module dip switches are 2000, 4000, and 8000 pulses per mile.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||10-21-1999||12:26 PM||||I have that info at home because I recently installed an aftermarket electronic speedometer in my Batmobile. I'll post that info later tonight, unless someone else beats me to it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||10-21-1999||01:17 PM||||It depends where you get the pulses from. The VSS gives off 4000 p/m, but the speedometer divides this by two (for the mathematically challenged, this comes to 2000 p/m   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). This signal is then fed to the ECM.<p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 10-21-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-21-1999||01:53 PM||||It would seem to me you will get a cleaner signal using the ECM feed. might make a more reliable setup.<P>What did the JCW kit come with for controls? Does it give you the new signal stalk or is it some thing you clamp/stick to the dash some place?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||10-22-1999||06:35 AM||||Hi,<P>The kit comes with a new signal stalk that looks exactly like the factory unit.  It also comes with the wiring harness, servo, brackets, and a bunch of fasteners and cable end fittings.  The instructions seem pretty complete so far.<BR>It does not come with the vacuum canister.  The instructions say that it is recommended on lower displacement engines but does not describe what they mean by lower displacement.<BR>It takes signals from the Tach, VSS speed sensor, and brake light circuit.  The Tach signal disengages the unit if the clutch is disengaged while the cruise is in service by looking at the rate of rise in rpm (what a mouthfull).  My clutch nuteral switch has long been gone so I'm glad I don't have to re-install one.<BR>I'll let you know what I think of the unit after I get the engine back in and get everything hooked up and working.<BR>The reason I need the pulse per mile info is that I need to set ten dip switches on the unit that describe the VSS signal, trans and engine size to the unit.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-22-1999||09:36 AM||||you should probably add the vac reserve can. Most motors I've seen, even big ones, have dropouts in the amount of vacuum available. Can be quite annoying.....<P>You can get a vacuum canister at nearly any junk yard for next to nothing. try to find one of the plastic ball type. they don't rust. make sure there is a working check valve in the one port. that port goes to manifold vacuum.... a working valve will let you suck air out but not blow into the canister. On the plastic ones it's not always obvious which port has the valve. Size of the canister shouldn't matter much, most run 4-6 inches in size. they are small enough to hide it over by the vapor can. you can then run the new lines right allong the vapor can lines.<P>There should be a extra vac port on the manifold. use that one. if there isn't a manifold port either spare, or on you can T into, the use the large line going to the vapor can. Assuming the vapor can is connected right, the large line is manifold vac.<P>if you bolt the cruise unit to the trunk wall on the empty bracket thats there it will look about stock when you are finished.||||reg||1
